WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:09.840
Hi everyone, it's Jerry. This is a Round 11
game from the 2016 Tata Steel Chess Tournament,

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:17.140
Master's section, between Magnus Carlsen playing
on the white end, and Hou Yifan. These two

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:22.940
played for the first time in last year's Tata
Steel Tournament where Carlsen had the white

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:28.910
pieces. He's playing as white again in this
one. Let's see how things play out. e4 e5

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:38.960
knight f3, knight f6, the Petrov's Defense.
We have the Nimzovich Attack with knight c3.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:45.650
Don't be mislead by the term 'attack'. We
have anything but that in this game. Things

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:57.260
are pretty uneventful in the opening and middlegame.
Several piece exchanges soon occur, and we

00:00:57.260 --> 00:01:01.899
have an ending in this one soon coming about
where it's queen and bishop versus queen and

00:01:01.899 --> 00:01:10.670
bishop. And shortly thereafter a king and
pawn ending alone. That part of the game is

00:01:10.670 --> 00:01:18.890
the part that I found interesting. Knight
takes, d takes. Minor pieces out, getting

00:01:18.890 --> 00:01:28.590
castled. White goes queenside, black prepares
queenside castle. b3, h3 looks to grab some

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:35.700
kingside space and complete development. Black
goes queenside. If black is going kingside

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:45.310
this is a much different ballgame. g4 a5 g5
each side going after the kings. Looking to

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:53.420
open files against the enemy king. Timing
will be a great factor. Not the case here.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:02:03.880
We're not attacking one another's king. Queenside
castle, g4 h6 rules out g5. If g5 is played,

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:09.450
this is a favorable exchange for white* (black).
The h-pawn has been exchanged for the extremely

00:02:09.450 --> 00:02:18.700
valuable g-pawn. White now has split pawns.
Bishop g2, king b8, white centralizes. So

00:02:18.700 --> 00:02:28.150
too does black. King b2, a6. And now white
prepares to double on the e-file, the only

00:02:28.150 --> 00:02:35.370
open file. And when that is the case, when
there is only one open file, it's not uncommon

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:43.129
for both sets of rooks to be exchanged. We
soon have both rooks exchanged in this one.

00:02:43.129 --> 00:02:52.290
Queen e7, this seems a little bit awkward
to place the queen opposite the rook. But

00:02:52.290 --> 00:02:59.310
this is only temporary. The queen's destination
is f8, and what she's basically doing...what

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:03.599
the queen is doing here is getting out of
the way of the light-squared bishop who had

00:03:03.599 --> 00:03:10.849
no good moves from at this point here. So
the queen is getting out of the bishop's way

00:03:10.849 --> 00:03:19.849
so he can move, and then the rooks can kind
of be offset. White doubles, queen f8, knight

00:03:19.849 --> 00:03:29.879
d4. Several exchanges now. Knight takes, bishop
takes, bishop takes. How to recapture on d4?

00:03:29.879 --> 00:03:36.959
Queen takes. You could go with the pawn. You're
not going to see a great shift in the evaluation.

00:03:36.959 --> 00:03:43.050
And this is something I'd like to draw just
a little bit of attention to...this capture.

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:50.370
Because I believe many would just go with
the pawn. Seeing this as a moment to ya know...this

00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:59.750
is my moment to undouble my pawns...let's
go for it. Queen takes is a move that I like.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:09.970
I like having these pawns close to the king.
With the queens still on the board like this...this

00:04:09.970 --> 00:04:15.030
configuration of pawns...and it doesn't show
up in this ending but it's worthwhile to note.

00:04:15.030 --> 00:04:21.619
It could very well end up in plenty of other
endgame situations where you're still working...each

00:04:21.619 --> 00:04:29.710
side is still working with a queen. This configuration
of pawns...the b3 c3 and c2 structure for

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:45.080
the white king is an excellent pocket to combat
a possible perpetual check by black. The b-pawn

00:04:45.080 --> 00:04:51.559
stops any checks along the file, the c-pawn
is responsible for stopping the diagonal checks,

00:04:51.559 --> 00:04:59.039
and the c-pawn stops rank checks. Compare
this with black, and their pawn structure.

00:04:59.039 --> 00:05:05.869
They don't have that configuration. We could
one day...suppose these guys are no longer

00:05:05.869 --> 00:05:14.430
around on the back rank. We could see the
queen bouncing back and forth on h8 and d4

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:24.419
to land a perpetual check to the king. Something
worthwhile to note. This is an asset in a

00:05:24.419 --> 00:05:30.990
queen ending...this type of pocket for the
white king. It is not the same if this pawn

00:05:30.990 --> 00:05:39.119
is here because he requires now the defense
of a piece other than the king. Anyhow, again

00:05:39.119 --> 00:05:43.990
it's not something that shows up in this game...this
idea of a perpetual check, but it's worthwhile

00:05:43.990 --> 00:05:53.139
to note. Okay, bishop d7. White grabs some
kingside space. Rooks are now exchanged, and

00:05:53.139 --> 00:06:02.610
there's really not...there hasn't been much
going on in this game. It's pretty even. When

00:06:02.610 --> 00:06:08.259
I bring up the graph, I'll show you...it'll
be saying that it's an ever so slight advantage.

00:06:08.259 --> 00:06:15.020
But it could just as well be saying zero for
an evaluation. There isn't...there isn't much

00:06:15.020 --> 00:06:22.199
to do. These next couple moves are examples
of just one move threats. Threatening mate

00:06:22.199 --> 00:06:29.889
in one. These one move threats are just not
enough. They're easily addressed. It's when

00:06:29.889 --> 00:06:37.199
there are two weaknesses, and if white is
able to pinpoint both weaknesses in one move...that

00:06:37.199 --> 00:06:41.979
can be difficult to defend. But these one
move threats are again...easily addressed.

00:06:41.979 --> 00:06:51.639
So, c6 defends. Bishop f1 threatens to take
a pawn since the b-pawn is pinned. Black gets

00:06:51.639 --> 00:06:58.339
out of the pin. And just kind of fast forwarding
some...this is really all we have are these

00:06:58.339 --> 00:07:04.169
one move threats. Queen attacks the pawn.
Counterattack against the bishop. The queen

00:07:04.169 --> 00:07:08.929
is now here to secure these two points. What
are the only other sensitive points? It's

00:07:08.929 --> 00:07:18.429
d6...the king will easily cover that. Bishops
are now off, and yeah. These are two vulnerable

00:07:18.429 --> 00:07:29.229
points...the king can watch over here. The
queen has the other two. c4, king up, check,

00:07:29.229 --> 00:07:38.179
and soon we have with this one the queens
being exchanged right here. And this is the

00:07:38.179 --> 00:07:46.800
big moment in the game. Extremely important
moment for black. Black's on move here...move

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:55.879
45. Up until this point dead even just call
it. This next move there's an issue however.

00:07:55.879 --> 00:08:04.610
There are 11 legal moves for black here. Only
one of them will hold this ending to a draw.

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:10.899
I wonder if you can spot it. What would you
do in this position as black? If you'd like

00:08:10.899 --> 00:08:15.959
to...go ahead spend some time with this
one working it out. Try and find the drawing

00:08:15.959 --> 00:08:29.080
idea for black in this king and pawn ending.
Okay, the drawing idea is a5. This is a must

00:08:29.080 --> 00:08:39.500
move. This was not played in the game. The
move played was h5, and this allows white

00:08:39.500 --> 00:08:46.889
to win. If I'm to just show the graph here...we
get an idea of how things played out all the

00:08:46.889 --> 00:08:54.410
way up until move 45. Again, it'll be showing
a slight advantage, but this is nothing slight...ya

00:08:54.410 --> 00:09:03.920
know...nothing significant I should say. Up
until this point king takes c3, .41. This

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:14.050
is a draw up until this point. An issue with
the next move h5, clearly. Let's see how things

00:09:14.050 --> 00:09:22.259
played out after h5. How did white enter...well
how did white make progress? He entered by way

00:09:22.259 --> 00:09:33.910
of b4 a5 and he really wants to get to b6.
Can he get there? Yes. Because he has these

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:41.970
tempi in reserve. He can eventually force
black into zugzwang...into a position where

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:47.690
he kind of has to concede something. He has
to give ground. Black has to give up control

00:09:47.690 --> 00:09:54.500
of b6. And this is a big deal. If the white
king could post up here, he's exerting pressure

00:09:54.500 --> 00:10:01.149
on both black pawns, and tied in with this
b5 break, black has to give up both the a

00:10:01.149 --> 00:10:11.959
and c pawn. So from here we had h4. King goes
back already. If d4 is played, well white

00:10:11.959 --> 00:10:18.970
still has another move here, b4 and then the
king has to go back. So in the game black

00:10:18.970 --> 00:10:28.269
just went back right away. King b6, and now
this is the idea...b5 takes takes, takes again,

00:10:28.269 --> 00:10:36.199
king c7, and after c3 black (Hou Yifan) resigned. There's
nothing to do here. Superior king position.

00:10:36.199 --> 00:10:43.259
What's soon to happen is the c and b pawn
will be exchanged, and then white could simply

00:10:43.259 --> 00:10:51.019
walk along the 5th rank and pick up all the
other black pawns. Suppose king d7, king b6,

00:10:51.019 --> 00:10:57.319
king c8, c6, takes takes, and then you get
the idea. There might have been one cute finish

00:10:57.319 --> 00:11:04.480
if things played out. If they took it further...we
could have had a...an interesting ending where

00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:12.269
after c6, king b8. This is still winning for
white. c takes b, still winning. Black king

00:11:12.269 --> 00:11:18.009
can't move. There's only one legal move with
d4. If you take it's stalemate, but you can

00:11:18.009 --> 00:11:23.970
actually push and allow black to queen first,
but in the end you're mating with a pawn.

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:31.860
This did not happen...again after c3 black
(Hou Yifan) resigned. So what was the drawing

00:11:31.860 --> 00:11:40.160
idea? It was a5, but why? Why does a5 draw,
and nothing else draw? Well what's the trick

00:11:40.160 --> 00:11:45.709
with this? a5 first off...it stops king b4,
but really the deeper idea, and the main idea

00:11:45.709 --> 00:11:57.209
here with a5 is to stop the king from entering
on b6. This is what a5 prevents white from

00:11:57.209 --> 00:12:01.870
doing. You cannot get your king to b6. If
you can't get to b6, you're not winning this

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:07.870
game. The only other key square...b6 is a
key square, it's a potential entry point for

00:12:07.870 --> 00:12:14.589
the white king...so too is e5. If white ever
tries to get to e5, well that's easily prevented

00:12:14.589 --> 00:12:23.420
by king e6. And notice that black has two
squares that he can continually move to. He

00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:28.209
can bounce back and forth on e6 and f6. And
what is he doing constantly? Watching over

00:12:28.209 --> 00:12:41.560
the key square e5. After a5, b4...suppose
b4. This is the idea by black...keep the pawn

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:49.550
on a5 sitting. h5, takes...if you're not taking
the pawn and playing here, you're not making

00:12:49.550 --> 00:12:55.749
any progress on this side of the board now,
and the only key square to watch over is e5,

00:12:55.749 --> 00:13:04.399
and that's easily guarded. If we take stock
of all the squares the black pawns control...b4

00:13:04.399 --> 00:13:13.310
c4 e4 d5. The white king can never go here...that's
another square, another one. This is the only

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:18.410
entry, and again it's easily guarded. So b5
doesn't do anything. The only thing to test

00:13:18.410 --> 00:13:29.639
out is this capture. After let's say h4, king
b4, king c7. a6 is the only try to open up

00:13:29.639 --> 00:13:37.709
the a5 square. But notice something...after
a6, black replies...black's reply I should

00:13:37.709 --> 00:13:45.439
say...does two things. One, captures a pawn...okay
we see that, but two...it opens up a new square

00:13:45.439 --> 00:13:55.430
for the black king...b7. So after a5, there's
king b7, and what has...what has transpired

00:13:55.430 --> 00:14:02.680
here? Well with this new square opened up,
black has...similar to this idea over here...when

00:14:02.680 --> 00:14:08.259
trying to watch over the e5 square. Black
has two squares he can move back and forth

00:14:08.259 --> 00:14:14.689
to, and always be controlling the key square
b6. Just as he was doing over here when the

00:14:14.689 --> 00:14:22.509
white king tries to enter the e5 square. The
king can continually move...there's endless...endless

00:14:22.509 --> 00:14:28.189
tempi. An infinite amount of tempi that the
king could use, and you're always watching

00:14:28.189 --> 00:14:35.779
these key squares. And this is...this is really
the trick here with this a5 idea. Ya know

00:14:35.779 --> 00:14:44.189
if white is going to enter in this direction,
they're going to have to take, and then eventually

00:14:44.189 --> 00:14:51.410
do this a6 push to open up the a5 square.
But you open up the a5 square for the king...after

00:14:51.410 --> 00:14:57.279
b takes a, there's now a new square opened
up for the black king. What happened in the

00:14:57.279 --> 00:15:04.670
game? We got to this point here where there
is, unfortunately for black, not two squares

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:12.529
for the king to continually bounce back and
forth to to watch over b6. That's the big

00:15:12.529 --> 00:15:21.100
trick here. This a5 idea eventually...the
tempos are all exhausted by black, and he

00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:30.389
has to concede ground...he has to give up
the b6 square. He's in zugzwang. a5 idea eliminates

00:15:30.389 --> 00:15:36.850
this little trick here. It doesn't allow white
to place black in zugzwang...playing this

00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:43.029
a5 idea. These endgames are tricky. King and
pawn endings, there's a lot of calculation

00:15:43.029 --> 00:15:52.180
needed. You have to be extremely careful with
how you use your tempi, and one wrong slip

00:15:52.180 --> 00:16:01.079
as we saw in this one...playing h5 instead
of a5, and the game was thrown away. Anyhow...I

00:16:01.079 --> 00:16:08.889
hope you found...well not the opening or middlegame
but this king and pawn ending instructive.

00:16:08.889 --> 00:16:12.980
And as usual...if you have any comments you'd
